# Stack profile — the only place that knows how this project builds and tests.
# `factory.sh setup` prefills it from the pipeline's presets (one file per build tool, browser runner
# or formatter it can detect); `setup --check` later names what the project gained since. No preset
# is read at run time: this file is the contract. Flat `key: value` pairs. A command that is not declared here is skipped by the
# gate and named in its report; it fails a stage only when `required:` names it. Every value is run through a
# shell, so a stack that needs two commands chains them (`a && b`) — except the command
# the gate appends a single-test selector to (`e2eTest:`, or `test:` when there is no
# `e2eTest:`), which stays one command, because the selector would reach only the last.
#
# compile      makes the test sources compile without running them
# test         runs the unit tests
# e2eTest      runs the end-user tests (falls back to `test` when absent). The suite starts the application
#              itself, in the test process and on a free port: a suite that drives an application started
#              beforehand makes the gate's red and green checks depend on whatever answers on that address
# filterFlag   the flag that selects a single test
# filterFormat how a selector `<Class>#<method>` is written for this runner. `{class}` and
#              `{method}` are the selector's halves; `{file}` is the source file the gate located
#              the test in, for a runner that selects by path — pytest: `"{file}::{method}"`
# testReport  where this runner leaves its report of what it executed, as a glob. Found by
#              convention when absent: JUnit XML (`build/test-results/**`, `surefire-reports/*`)
#              and TRX (`TestResults/**`). Any stack can emit one of those two — pytest with
#              `--junitxml`, jest with jest-junit, `gotestsum --junitfile`, `cargo nextest`,
#              PHPUnit, RSpec, the .NET platform with `--logger trx` — so naming the path is all
#              a new stack needs
# testEvidence `exit-code` to accept the weaker check where a runner writes no report at all. The
#              gate then says so on every single test, because an exit code cannot distinguish a
#              failing test from a runner that never ran one
# covers.<key> which tests a command runs, where its own text does not say: `**` for every
#              test in the project (`dotnet test` on a solution), or a comma-separated list
#              of path prefixes. Needed because whether a pathless command runs everything
#              or one source set is knowledge about that build tool — the gate refuses to
#              guess it, and running the wrong task would call a criterion covered that
#              nothing executes
# browser      the browser runner's name (`playwright`, or the one the project uses) when the end-user
#              tests drive a real browser (e2eTest runs them) — any value but `none` means "has one"; `none`
#              when the project deliberately has none. With `none` the plan may fall back to a lower
#              test level for a page; unset, a criterion only a browser can observe stops the plan
#              once for the stack decision. `factory.sh setup` sets it where it finds a Playwright setup
# integration  `none` when the project has decided to have no integration level; otherwise one
#              `test.<name>:` source set runs the scenarios below the page (contract 9: every scenario but
#              the happy path and a browser-only one is integrated). Neither declared: the plan stops once
# test.journey runs the journey tests — guards over an epic's delivered stories, walked to the epic's
#              outcome event. They need the running application, so like `e2eTest` they stay out of
#              `required:` and run in CI
# http.stub    the stub an integration test puts in place of an external system, at the protocol:
#              `wiremock` (Java), `wiremock-net` (.NET) or `none`. Never a mock of the port whose adapter
#              the story changes, never a shared or real instance. `setup --check` proposes it where it
#              finds the dependency; `dca-add http-stub` sets it up
# architecture runs the architecture rule suite — the gate runs it in the build stage
# format       runs the formatter check — the gate runs it in the build and tidy stages
# formatFix    corrects the formatting (`./gradlew spotlessApply`, `dotnet format`). The stages that write
#              code — test, build, tidy — run it before they finish; the gate and the commit hook run
#              only `format:` and change no file
# carrier.<r>  the skill that carries a stage's craft or a role — `carrier.plan`, `carrier.test`,
#              `carrier.build`, `carrier.tidy`, `carrier.guard` (the invariants build and tidy keep),
#              `carrier.glossary`, `carrier.domain` (the designed context map); the list is open. A
#              skill works in every tool; an agent only where a tool has them. `factory.sh setup`
#              writes a line only for a skill installed beside the pipeline — a named carrier that is
#              missing stops a run — and leaves the rest commented out, found by description
# knowledge    the ONE skill a stage may treat as an authority, with citations. Name it only when
#              the project vouches for it being current: a vendored catalog from an older release
#              names rules and markers that no longer exist, and a citation makes that look verified.
#              Unnamed means the stages work from the project's own rules, markers and documents
# model.<tool>.<stage>  the model <tool> (claude, codex, opencode) runs <stage> on (plan, test,
#              build, tidy, judge, document); model.<tool> is that tool's default for every stage
#              without a key of its own. Values are the tool's own model names or aliases. Always
#              bound to a tool: a profile is shared, and a model name means nothing to another tool —
#              the gate refuses an unqualified model key. Unset: the tool's default. A --model/-m
#              in FACTORY_<TOOL>_ARGS overrides it for one person, and the run says so
# contract     the gate file contract this profile was written for (see below)
# sessionUsage `off` to keep the gate out of the tools' session logs (~/.claude, ~/.codex): stages run
#              inside a session are then recorded as unknown. Default on; FACTORY_SESSION_USAGE=off
#              switches it off for one person
# required     the checks that must hold for any change: `compile`, `architecture`, `format` and
#              each test command by its own key (`test`, `test.<name>`, `e2eTest`) — an end-user
#              suite that needs a running system can stay out of it. `story-gate.py --change` (the commit hook, CI, a direct edit) then fails
#              a required check that is not declared, not run in its scope, or ran no test at all.
#              Absent: nothing is mandatory — a declared check that runs red fails, an undeclared one is named.
#              The build and tidy gates also run the required test commands whole, so a story
#              cannot break what the stories before it delivered. A build tool that skips an
#              up-to-date task writes no new report — give a required test command its rerun flag
#              (Gradle: `./gradlew test --rerun`), since only a report from this run counts
# product      the product description (default: project/product.md) — what is built, for whom, its
#              surfaces, how it works, how it looks. Mandatory before the first story, with `tech`
# tech         the technical description (default: project/tech.md) — stack, frontend approach,
#              persistence, runtime, integrations, version policy
# domain       the designed context map (default: project/domain.md) — contexts, subdomain types,
#              relationships and why. Optional; read first by the plan gate's context check
#              The three are written with the person by /factory-setup (through the project's
#              description skill); `setup` writes a key only where the project instructions name
#              another place. A key that names no file fails the gate
# backlog      where the epics and stories are (default: project/backlog)
# contextMap   the context map generated from the code, "as built" (found by convention when absent);
#              a difference to `domain` is a finding, not a duplicate
# glossary     file name of a bounded context's glossary (default: glossary.md anywhere)
# acceptance   `none` (default), `pages` or `all`: which stories wait for a human's look before they
#              are delivered. `pages` holds a story with a criterion the end-user command tests in a
#              project with a browser. The document gate then writes an acceptance record instead of
#              delivering; "accepted" delivers, a correction goes into the same story, which runs
#              again from plan. /factory-setup writes `pages` where the product has web pages
# run          how a person starts the application to look at it (`./gradlew bootRun`,
#              `dotnet run --project src/App.Web`) — named in the acceptance record
# reviews      perspectives the judge stage covers *in addition to* its built-in ones
# review.<p>   the reviewer that should carry one perspective. A review *skill* works in every
#              tool; a review *agent* only in a tool that has agents — where it is missing, the
#              judge runs the built-in pass itself and says so

# Which gate file contract this profile is written for. The gate refuses a run when the number is
# higher than the gate it is asked to run against — the project would be relying on keys that copy
# of the script does not know, and a key ignored in silence is a check that has quietly gone.
contract: 9

compile: {{COMPILE_COMMAND}}
test: {{TEST_COMMAND}}
e2eTest: {{E2E_TEST_COMMAND}}
filterFlag: {{FILTER_FLAG}}
filterFormat: {{FILTER_FORMAT}}
architecture: {{ARCHITECTURE_COMMAND}}
format: {{FORMAT_COMMAND}}
formatFix: {{FORMAT_FIX_COMMAND}}
#required: compile test architecture
#acceptance: pages
#run: <how a person starts the application>
#carrier.test: <the skill that carries this project's end-user testing craft>
#carrier.build: <the skill that carries this project's implementation craft>
#carrier.guard: <the skill that holds the invariants while build and tidy edit>
#carrier.glossary: <the skill that keeps the glossary>
#carrier.domain: <the skill that keeps the designed context map>
# A perspective is found by description when a matching review skill is installed; name a carrier
# here only to override that choice or to bind an added perspective.
# Which stages can run on a cheaper model without the judge finding more: fill in only what the
# project has measured (factory.sh status --story <story> shows the model and the cost per stage).
#model.claude.tidy: <model>
#model.claude.document: <model>
#reviews: security, performance
#review.security: <the review skill that carries it>
